<h2>The Three Core Elements and How They Work</h2>
<p>There are many harness components, but the instructor takes the view that <strong>multi-agent collaboration happens well with just these three</strong> and focuses on them &mdash; <strong>agent · skill · orchestrator</strong>.</p>

<h3>1) Agent — a natural-language document</h3>
<p>An agent is not code but <strong>a document written in natural language</strong>. It holds the following.</p>
<ul>
    <li>The role and the <strong>principles</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Input protocol · output protocol</strong> (in what format to produce the result)</li>
    <li><strong>Collaboration</strong> &mdash; what to exchange with other agents</li>
    <li>Behavior on re-invocation and <strong>when an error occurs</strong></li>
</ul>

<h3>2) Skill — standardizing repeated work</h3>
<p>A skill is a <strong>name + description</strong> that freely holds a defined process, knowledge and checklist.</p>
<ul>
    <li>Because the process is defined, <strong>consistency</strong> is maintained.</li>
    <li>You can <strong>combine</strong> small skills to compose a bigger system.</li>
    <li>Thanks to the standardization, repeated work gets faster.</li>
</ul>
<p>A knack for designing skills: <strong>find the repeated work → separate it by single responsibility → define input/output → handle exceptions → connect it to an agent.</strong></p>

<h3>3) Orchestrator — a PM that does not micromanage</h3>
<p>It is the <strong>PM</strong> responsible for whether the whole workflow is carried out well. Unlike a failing PM it does not interfere in the details; it only checks something like "is the workflow being carried out well".</p>

